    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00335:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Step 1 of [[xref:fig-00002]]: Addition of Thiamin Diphosphate"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00002",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00335:title:sect-00002-title",
            "text": "MECHANISM"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00003",
              "segmentId": "m00335:para:para-00003"
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00004",
              "segmentId": "m00335:para:para-00004"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00001",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00335:alt:fig-00001-alt",
                  "text": "Thiamin diphosphate reacts with base to form thiamin diphosphate ylide. The thiazolium ring's p K a is 18. Pyruvate and yilde consolidation  yields an alpha hydroxyl acid thiamin diphosphate."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_29_06_001.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00002",
              "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
              "caption": {
                "segmentId": "m00335:caption:fig-00002-caption"
              },
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00335:alt:fig-00002-alt",
                  "text": "The conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-coenzyme A involves thiamin diphosphate ylide condensation, decarboxylation, addition of lipoamide, and coenzyme A nucleophilic acyl substitution."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_29_06_002.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00003",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00335:title:sect-00003-title",
            "text": "Step 2 of [[xref:fig-00002]]: Decarboxylation"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00005",
              "segmentId": "m00335:para:para-00005"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00003",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00335:alt:fig-00003-alt",
                  "text": "Thiamin addition product undergoes decarboxylation reaction that leads to the formation of hydroxyethylthiamin diphosphate and carbon dioxide as the product."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_29_06_003.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00004",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00335:title:sect-00004-title",
            "text": "Step 3 of [[xref:fig-00002]]: Reaction with Lipoamide"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00006",
              "segmentId": "m00335:para:para-00006"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00004",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00335:alt:fig-00004-alt",
                  "text": "Hydroxyethylthiamin diphosphate reacts with lipoamide to form a hemithioacetal product. The square bracket parentheses show the lipoamide structure in which lipoic acid and lysine are labeled."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_29_06_004.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00005",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00335:title:sect-00005-title",
            "text": "Step 4 of [[xref:fig-00002]]: Elimination of Thiamin Diphosphate"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00007",
              "segmentId": "m00335:para:para-00007"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00005",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00335:alt:fig-00005-alt",
                  "text": "Base deprotonation of a hemithioacetal decomposes to form acetyl dihydrolipoamide and T P P ylide."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_29_06_005.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00006",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00335:title:sect-00006-title",
            "text": "Step 5 of [[xref:fig-00002]]: Acyl Transfer"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00008",
              "segmentId": "m00335:para:para-00008"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00006",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00335:alt:fig-00006-alt",
                  "text": "Acetyldihydrolipamide undergoes nucleophilic acyl substitution with coenzyme A to yield acetyl CoA and dihydrolipoamide. Dihydrolipoamide is then reduced by flavin adenine dinucleotide to a lipoamide."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_29_06_006.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
